            "#marketplace"
          ],
          "text": "**Architecture approved 2026-07-19 (roadmap-only, no code yet)** \u2014 design in `~/.claude/plans/the-arch-guard-trusts-effervescent-moonbeam.md` (Track B) + memory `project_sprig_marketplace.md`. **Spine = catalog-as-data.** The one hard blocker: `SprigSupervisor.CATALOG` is a hardcoded Python dict (`supervisor.py` ~222-625). Extract the `_sprig()` entry schema to a validated model and merge entries from three sources at runtime, all feeding the _unchanged_ graft pipeline (allowlist \u2192 arch guard \u2192 pull \u2192 sha256 \u2192 minisign \u2192 extract \u2192 spawn). The marketplace is a new *source* of catalog entries, not a new trust model. **Three lanes = a trust ladder, not a trust hole:** (S1) first-party curated signed index; (S2) curated community, publisher-signed via the existing per-entry `pubkey`; (S3) admin self-load \u2014 any OCI ref added at runtime, persisted to the volume, graftable with no image rebuild. S3 is the escape hatch, the sprig-dev inner loop and the community on-ramp at once; `SPRIG_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1` ratchets an instance to signed-only. **Phases:** M1 catalog-as-data + admin self-load (keystone, recommended first slice; also redacts `repo/tag/binary_sha256/insecure` from the browser catalog); M2 remote storefront + in-house registry cutover (Zot); M3 discovery UX; M4 community submission; M5 commerce \u2014 paid sprigs + checkout issuing a signed entitlement enforced two ways. Open: storefront name, M1 index shape, self-load sha-pin default, Zot vs GHCR-proxy, entitlement binding."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "96d4d4ba2e8c38dd",
      "line_num": 218,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bonsai",
        "#marketplace"
      ],
      "text": "**Sprig\u2122 marketplace \u2014 simple selling of Sprigs (captured 2026-07-08, now a PUBLIC commitment: the sage.is/bonsai FAQ says \"A Sprig marketplace is on our roadmap to make selling one simple\"): support paid and proprietary Sprigs with listing/selling as simple as Etsy made it for makers \u2014 Alexander (CTO) was a founding engineer there (employee #5), and that pedigree is named on the page. Interacts with: the in-house registry cutover (paid artifacts need authenticated pulls), catalog schema enrichment (license/tier/price surface), and the spec's `delivery: service-endpoint` shape (hosted paid Sprigs need no artifact download at all). Methodology inspiration (both named by Alexander 2026-07-08): Etsy for maker-simple selling, and Shopify's playbook for getting devs involved \u2014 a partner/developer program, first-class SDKs and docs (the MIT `sage-is-sprig-sdk-py` sketched in the Sprig Spec is the seed), revenue share that favors the builder, and the marketplace itself as the distribution channel that makes writing a Sprig worth a developer's weekend. Scope TBD: payments, publisher identity/verification, revenue split, and how a purchased Sprig lands in an operator's catalog.**\n- [ ] **Architecture approved 2026-07-19 (roadmap-only, no code yet)** \u2014 design in `~/.claude/plans/the-arch-guard-trusts-effervescent-moonbeam.md` (Track B) + memory `project_sprig_marketplace.md`. **Spine = catalog-as-data.** The one hard blocker: `SprigSupervisor.CATALOG` is a hardcoded Python dict (`supervisor.py` ~222-625). Extract the `_sprig()` entry schema to a validated model and merge entries from three sources at runtime, all feeding the _unchanged_ graft pipeline (allowlist \u2192 arch guard \u2192 pull \u2192 sha256 \u2192 minisign \u2192 extract \u2192 spawn). The marketplace is a new *source* of catalog entries, not a new trust model. **Three lanes = a trust ladder, not a trust hole:** (S1) first-party curated signed index; (S2) curated community, publisher-signed via the existing per-entry `pubkey`; (S3) admin self-load \u2014 any OCI ref added at runtime, persisted to the volume, graftable with no image rebuild. S3 is the escape hatch, the sprig-dev inner loop and the community on-ramp at once; `SPRIG_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1` ratchets an instance to signed-only. **Phases:** M1 catalog-as-data + admin self-load (keystone, recommended first slice; also redacts `repo/tag/binary_sha256/insecure` from the browser catalog); M2 remote storefront + in-house registry cutover (Zot); M3 discovery UX; M4 community submission; M5 commerce \u2014 paid sprigs + checkout issuing a signed entitlement enforced two ways. Open: storefront name, M1 index shape, self-load sha-pin default, Zot vs GHCR-proxy, entitlement binding. #bonsai #marketplace\n\n`#bonsai` `#marketplace`\n`TODO.md:L218`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3750
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "18b42c05aef3d865",
      "line_num": 221,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bonsai",
        "#agents"
      ],
      "text": "**Incoming: `startr-team` agent framework as a `service-endpoint` Sprig (midterm; awareness notice from Scion 2026-07-11, no action requested). It grafts onto AI-UI as a REMOTE Sprig, never as a source dependency \u2014 brand boundary is Sage-is = science+platform, Startr = tooling+product, and the arms-length Graft Union\u2122 settles licensing. Done now, at the cheap moment: `agent-` capability prefix RESERVED in sprig-spec v1.md with a planned `sage-is/v1/agent` dispatch shape; CHANGELOG [Unreleased] entry added. Midterm work when the Sprig lands: (a) define the contract \u2014 run lifecycle, streamed events, cancellation, schema at `sprig-spec/schemas/sage-is/v1/agent.json`; (b) explicit rootstock dispatch wiring, since the \"no per-capability dispatch code\" guarantee covers only OpenAI-compatible shapes; (c) `delivery: service-endpoint` support, currently unimplemented. Decisions locked 2026-07-11 \u2014 these are the EXTENDED tier; simple agents already exist as workspace Agents and Space agents: (1) bidirectional from day one, so the contract includes a rootstock callback surface and agents work with the operator's data instead of shipping copies; (2) both deliveries \u2014 `service-endpoint` hosted, `oci-artifact` on-hardware, keeping zero-egress intact; (3) one tiered \"Agents\" surface, no renames, and the model-transparency claim extends to the remote tier; (4) job queue + spend budgets are PREREQUISITES \u2014 runs live on the queue, budgets gate spend. Scion's pointer paths were stale: specs live in the BONSAI sibling repos, the graft API is `routers/sprigs.py`, pins live in the supervisor CATALOG.**\n`#bonsai` `#agents`\n`TODO.md:L221`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3840
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "93cadf4a8593c9a4",
      "line_num": 223,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#bonsai",
        "#site"
      ],
      "text": "**Author the bonsai/sprigs pages as markdown + a text-graph\u2192SVG diagram pipeline (captured 2026-07-08): both pages are hand-written njk today; re-author them as markdown so content edits stop requiring template surgery. Diagrams from text markup rendered to SVG at build time \u2014 mermaid is the baseline candidate, or a custom \"simple text graph\" markup (TBD, mermaid-style) whose renderer emits house-styled SVG (startr.style tokens, correct fonts, label collision avoidance \u2014 the Graft Union\u2122 label landing on a connector line is exactly the class of bug a renderer should prevent). Candidate consumers beyond these two pages: the bonsai.sage.is spec hub and future architecture docs.**\n`#bonsai` `#site`\n`TODO.md:L223`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3930
    },
