      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "74c8f87a816028c5",
      "line_num": 328,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Audio/TTS UX polish (surfaced live in dev mode 2026-07-26; the last two ride with the Settings/Admin migration pass):**\n- [x] First-playback Kokoro load was silent for seconds \u2192 now a dismissible \"Loading the Kokoro voice model (first use)\u2026\" toast (`ResponseMessage.svelte`)\n- [x] Kokoro double-load in Settings (doubling progress %) \u2192 memoized load promise so `getVoices()` + the reactive share one download (`Settings/Audio.svelte`)\n- [ ] Opening Settings \u2192 Audio RELOADS Kokoro even after playback already loaded it \u2014 the Settings main-thread loader and the playback Web Worker (`kokoro.worker.ts`) are separate contexts; share one loader/model so the model loads once per session\n- [ ] Kokoro voice selector is janky (a type-in input + a list that only opens after you clear the field) \u2192 replace with a clean, DRY `<select>`/combobox\n\n`TODO.md:L328`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5280
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 378,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 379,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 380,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 381,
          "tags": [
            "#security",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 13/15, both failures pre-existing leaks; committed). The backend described permissions twice \u2014 env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS`, which `has_permission()` ENFORCES, against Pydantic model defaults, which the admin panel ADVERTISES \u2014 with nothing keeping them in step. Diffed inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (the `sharing.public_*` four, latent because persisted values masked them) and **1 key missing entirely**. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible \u2014 the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, so **no non-admin ever got the web-search button**. Fixes: added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`); the Pydantic defaults now DERIVE from the table, so a field with no entry raises `KeyError` at import rather than shipping a silent policy lie; `get_permissions()` backfills, so upgraded deployments get the key too. `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables, and is deliberately one-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries the retired `workspace` group. Measure-twice RED (2 of 3 failing) then GREEN, and verified against a real v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21)."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 382,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 383,
          "tags": [
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**RESOLVED \u2014 never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, `upgrade_gate` **21/21**). `writer | grep -q PATTERN` reports a MATCH as a FAILURE: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it read as flakiness. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 141 on a match, five runs of five). It cost three gates, and the third is the bad one: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback, where 141 reads as \"no match\" \u2014 so that check reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. Devices, in preference order: pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` (`text_has`, `log_has`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has`) that capture the text and match with bash's own operators; every unbounded-writer site converted (12); `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to fail on a reintroduced line; and `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves, because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint \u2014 only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, since `printf`/`echo` are builtins that finish before grep can exit."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 384,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 385,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 386,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 387,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 388,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 389,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 390,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 391,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26; `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known leaks failing; committed). Surveyed every layer that could take a cache, fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides the rest: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern caches *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated. **(1) Base-models cache.** `/api/models` probed every provider on every call: measured live, **~740 ms uncached against ~4.5 ms cached (~160\u00d7)**, with `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulting to `False`. Flipped the default, which also engages the existing startup warm. It had been off because the cache has no TTL and nothing cleared it, so `invalidate_base_models_cache()` now fires from both provider-config update endpoints. `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts both halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path; RED at 749 ms before, green after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 ETag only, so every repeat visit spent a revalidation round-trip. `CachedStaticFiles` sets a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`, because those filenames are reused across deploys. **Combined, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), `/api/models` 768 \u2192 17 ms.** Upgrade caveat: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so an admin who explicitly saved it keeps their choice."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 392,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 393,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#architecture"
          ],
          "text": "**Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 394,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits)"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 395,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 396,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e"
          ],
          "text": "**Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind, and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface before each spec and restores ONLY what changed, so specs that touch nothing issue zero writes and a leak is logged BY NAME rather than surfacing as whichever spec fails next. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine\" while merely inheriting whatever the container held; it sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself now, and is true in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 my original diagnosis was wrong.** The graft succeeded; `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matched nothing because grafting became restart-free and the note was reworded, so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. The assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog`, and the test is pinned `retries: 0` \u2014 a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test against different state."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 397,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (2026-07-29). `run-cypress.sh` did `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` then `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" ... || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container still going away holds its volume, the removal fails, and the run boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Third instance of one class \u2014 *a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success* \u2014 after `docker run` without `-i` and `| grep -q` under pipefail. The symptom is the dangerous part: it surfaced as `sprigs-panel` failing on a surface nobody had touched, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place and reads as a flake. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and hard-exit naming the container still holding it. Proved able to fire by pinning the volume with an unrelated container \u2014 `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`. Process note: do not run docker experiments beside a running gate."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 398,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 no, and the direction I called human-only never was** (Alexander, 2026-07-29). No WebDriver-family tool can open the OS colour dialog, because the browser renders it outside the page. But that dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that \u2014 `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and Svelte's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. Tested green against BOTH implementations and proved able to fail by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated, and it is fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) earns its place** is real input physics: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went 13/13 green under a real driver and was broken on a real trackpad. Keep it on the roadmap for the streaming and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134); it is close to worthless for form surfaces, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 399,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 400,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "b9c13c4e5c4ac63c",
      "line_num": 377,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phase Q \u2014 clear the decks: current-SPA drift fixes, cruft, and perf quick-wins (no migration needed).**\n- [x] Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed\n- [x] Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed\n- [x] Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed\n- [x] **Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 13/15, both failures pre-existing leaks; committed). The backend described permissions twice \u2014 env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS`, which `has_permission()` ENFORCES, against Pydantic model defaults, which the admin panel ADVERTISES \u2014 with nothing keeping them in step. Diffed inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (the `sharing.public_*` four, latent because persisted values masked them) and **1 key missing entirely**. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible \u2014 the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, so **no non-admin ever got the web-search button**. Fixes: added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`); the Pydantic defaults now DERIVE from the table, so a field with no entry raises `KeyError` at import rather than shipping a silent policy lie; `get_permissions()` backfills, so upgraded deployments get the key too. `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables, and is deliberately one-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries the retired `workspace` group. Measure-twice RED (2 of 3 failing) then GREEN, and verified against a real v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21). #security #critical\n- [ ] **Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control. #frontend #tech-debt\n- [x] **RESOLVED \u2014 never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, `upgrade_gate` **21/21**). `writer | grep -q PATTERN` reports a MATCH as a FAILURE: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it read as flakiness. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 141 on a match, five runs of five). It cost three gates, and the third is the bad one: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback, where 141 reads as \"no match\" \u2014 so that check reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. Devices, in preference order: pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` (`text_has`, `log_has`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has`) that capture the text and match with bash's own operators; every unbounded-writer site converted (12); `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to fail on a reintroduced line; and `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves, because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint \u2014 only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, since `printf`/`echo` are builtins that finish before grep can exit. #critical\n- [x] Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed.\n- [x] Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed\n- [x] Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf.\n- [x] Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering. #perf #frontend\n- [x] Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed. #perf #frontend\n- [x] **Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26; `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known leaks failing; committed). Surveyed every layer that could take a cache, fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides the rest: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern caches *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated. **(1) Base-models cache.** `/api/models` probed every provider on every call: measured live, **~740 ms uncached against ~4.5 ms cached (~160\u00d7)**, with `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulting to `False`. Flipped the default, which also engages the existing startup warm. It had been off because the cache has no TTL and nothing cleared it, so `invalidate_base_models_cache()` now fires from both provider-config update endpoints. `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts both halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path; RED at 749 ms before, green after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 ETag only, so every repeat visit spent a revalidation round-trip. `CachedStaticFiles` sets a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`, because those filenames are reused across deploys. **Combined, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), `/api/models` 768 \u2192 17 ms.** Upgrade caveat: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so an admin who explicitly saved it keeps their choice. #perf\n- [ ] **`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too. #perf #tech-debt\n- [ ] **Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers. #perf #architecture\n- [ ] Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits) #perf\n- [ ] Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size. #perf\n- [x] **Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind, and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface before each spec and restores ONLY what changed, so specs that touch nothing issue zero writes and a leak is logged BY NAME rather than surfacing as whichever spec fails next. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine\" while merely inheriting whatever the container held; it sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself now, and is true in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 my original diagnosis was wrong.** The graft succeeded; `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matched nothing because grafting became restart-free and the note was reworded, so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. The assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog`, and the test is pinned `retries: 0` \u2014 a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test against different state. #e2e\n- [x] **The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (2026-07-29). `run-cypress.sh` did `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` then `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" ... || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container still going away holds its volume, the removal fails, and the run boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Third instance of one class \u2014 *a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success* \u2014 after `docker run` without `-i` and `| grep -q` under pipefail. The symptom is the dangerous part: it surfaced as `sprigs-panel` failing on a surface nobody had touched, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place and reads as a flake. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and hard-exit naming the container still holding it. Proved able to fire by pinning the volume with an unrelated container \u2014 `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`. Process note: do not run docker experiments beside a running gate. #e2e #critical\n- [x] **\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 no, and the direction I called human-only never was** (Alexander, 2026-07-29). No WebDriver-family tool can open the OS colour dialog, because the browser renders it outside the page. But that dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that \u2014 `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and Svelte's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. Tested green against BOTH implementations and proved able to fail by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated, and it is fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) earns its place** is real input physics: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went 13/13 green under a real driver and was broken on a real trackpad. Keep it on the roadmap for the streaming and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134); it is close to worthless for form surfaces, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does. #e2e #frontend\n- [ ] Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched\n- [ ] Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate. #e2e #tech-debt\n\n`TODO.md:L377`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5370
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "3c6e821e288df1cf",
      "line_num": 415,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#ux"
      ],
      "text": "**Human UX review of the no-build surfaces before any of them takes over a route (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"it's close but the UX feels a little off\"). Two surfaces exist in both forms right now \u2014 `/admin/sprigs` vs `/pages/admin/sprigs`, `/admin/diagnostics` vs `/pages/admin/diagnostics` \u2014 which makes this the cheapest it will ever be to judge: same data, same deployment, flip between them. `scripts/manual-check.sh` boots a seeded instance with both live (`--graft-ui` for the marketplace slot). This is not a nice-to-have: Phase S established that a green suite is the WEAKEST evidence on an interactive surface (an autoscroll went 13/13 green and was broken on a real trackpad), and this phase has already produced a second instance of the same thing \u2014 the \"Show me how to fix this\" button rendered and did nothing, every automated assertion passed, and a human click found it in seconds. Known feel-differences to judge rather than assume: messages are CSS toasts fixed to the top instead of a toast library; the fix remedy is a `<details>` disclosure instead of a modal; the diagnostics timestamp is absolute instead of a live relative label; the Sprigs panel swaps whole rather than patching; the branding preview reflects SAVED values rather than updating as you type. Its colour pair is now tested in BOTH directions (see below), so what a human adds there is narrower than first filed: whether the swatch opens the OS dialog at all, and whether picking a colour in it feels immediate. Scope it as a real review with someone who has not been building it.**\n`#frontend` `#ux`\n`TODO.md:L415`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5460
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 418,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**SURVEYED: every migration candidate on one snapshot, and the sorting key was wrong** (2026-08-02; Alexander: _\"Given how well this worked let's think about what other routes would benefit from our approach.\"_). `cypress/e2e/upgrade/route-payload.cy.ts`, 10/10 green, 51 agents / 32 users / 17 knowledge bases / 8 prompts on the restored snapshot. **The finding is the FLOOR.** `/notes` for a user who owns none \u2014 notes are scoped per user and the snapshot's 14 belong to six other people \u2014 still costs **127 requests, 781 kB wire, 7,575 kB decoded**. That is what a route with *no data of its own* pays. Every route's own data measured against it: **prompts +121 kB, functions +206, users +296, knowledge +455, evaluations +1,269, agents +1,714, chat +5,170.** So for six of the eight, the list the surface exists to show is **2\u20136% of what loading it costs**, and ordering the migration by list size orders it by noise. **`/api/models` is 2,304 kB and it is fetched on every single route** \u2014 chat, notes, functions, users, all of them \u2014 and it is the only thing on the floor that is not cached: 741 kB of the 781 kB wire, **95%**, on a warm client. Someone opening `/admin/functions` to look at one function downloads 2.3 MB of model metadata. **The server-rendered page for comparison: 12 requests, 234 kB wire, 472 kB decoded \u2014 one sixteenth of the EMPTY SvelteKit route.** **Method notes, because they bound what these numbers mean**: single run per route on one machine, so decoded bytes are the ranking column (stable to 0.1 kB across three runs) and times are indicative only (knowledge came in at 1,425 ms and 3,241 ms on two runs); the shell is warm after the first route, so `wireKB` is a returning-visitor figure, not a cold one \u2014 `workshop-payload.cy.ts` holds the cold numbers. `/home` is **not** in the survey: Spaces has no list hook yet, and measuring it against a shell selector would have produced a plausible number for the wrong thing."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 419,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**The reordering that follows from it \u2014 one column is Alexander's, not mine.** Because the throughput win is within a few hundred kB of identical for every candidate, throughput can no longer break the tie; what is left is lines deleted and how often a route is opened. **There is no telemetry in this product, so the last column is judgement and stays empty until Alexander fills it.** Component lines, counted: **users 3,126 \u00b7 notes 2,910 \u00b7 knowledge 1,671 \u00b7 evaluations 1,359 \u00b7 functions 1,249 \u00b7 prompts 635** (Agents landed at 53% of its count, so treat these as ceilings). On that basis, and pending the frequency column: **`/admin/users` moves ahead of knowledge** \u2014 it is the largest component in the candidate set, it is the one surface whose base64 avatars are actually *rendered* rather than downloaded and dropped, and `users.cy.ts` already exists as its guard-rail. **`/notes` is a different animal and should not be judged on this table** \u2014 2,910 lines of editor, not a list, so the props-and-forms pattern that took Agents to zero JavaScript does not obviously apply. **`/workshop/prompts` is the cheap one**: 635 lines, 121 kB above the floor, and it would take an afternoon \u2014 worth doing as a warm-up, not as a win."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 420,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Floor, part 1 of 3 \u2014 fonts and icons: \u2212934 kB decoded on EVERY route, measured before and after** (2026-08-02; Alexander: _\"We could quickly do A and B.\"_). Three samples each side through `route-payload.cy.ts`, **spread 0.0 kB**, so the delta is the measurement rather than an impression: the floor went **7,576.0 \u2192 6,642.5 kB** and every SvelteKit route moved by the same \u2212934, agents-legacy by \u22121,056. The server-rendered pages moved 0.0 \u2014 they load none of it, which is the check that the instrument is reading what it claims. **Fonts.** Four of five `@font-face` rules shipped `.ttf`; `Mona-Sans.woff2` had been sitting beside them the whole time proving the toolchain could do better. Archivo **652 \u2192 187 kB (\u221271%)** and Vazirmatn **241 \u2192 111 kB**, by format conversion only \u2014 **not one glyph dropped**, so no locale audit was owed. Then a `unicode-range` scoping Vazirmatn to the Arabic blocks: it sat in the body stack as Archivo's fallback, so every reader in every locale was pulling 235 kB of Persian glyphs to render English. **It is now fetched on no route at all** unless Persian text is present. **Icons.** The `image/svg+xml` link is gone and nothing was lost: `favicon.svg` is 203 kB of **two 2036\u00d72040 PNGs base64-embedded in an SVG wrapper**, and the two embedded images are byte-identical (md5 `1dfc08a3b1e1`, 75,512 bytes each) \u2014 a `<style>` block switching light/dark between the same picture, preferred by browsers over the 13.6 kB PNG already declared beside it. The five 512\u00d7512 masters (`favicon`, `logo`, `splash` and both \"dark\" twins, all byte-identical to each other) are now 256\u00d7256 at **45,710 bytes**. **Why the asset and not the 27 call sites**: it is one cached URL today, so repointing some of them would make the browser fetch two files instead of one. Largest render anywhere is 5rem, the splash is 6rem, and the PWA manifest uses separate 192/512 files \u2014 nothing needed 512. `git checkout` reverts it if the art matters more than the bytes. **The honest limit**: these assets cache and `/api/models` does not, so this is a first-visit win, not a second-navigation one."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 421,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#dx"
          ],
          "text": "**The `unicode-range` got a two-sided guard, and writing it taught me the two directions need different assertions** (`cypress/e2e/font-unicode-range.cy.ts`). It can fail two ways and **only one is visible**: too narrow and Persian readers silently get fallback glyphs, which nobody here reads and which would ship; too wide and the 235 kB quietly returns. **Two wrong attempts, both worth keeping.** (1) Widening the range to include Latin did *not* re-introduce the download \u2014 `unicode-range` says which characters a face MAY serve, but a browser only fetches a face it actually USES, and Archivo comes first in the stack. (2) Deleting the range entirely did not either, on a page reachable without signing in. So the first draft's \"en-US does not download Vazirmatn\" **passed whether or not the change was present** \u2014 an assertion that cannot fail, reporting coverage it did not have. Rebuilt as structural for the too-wide direction (assert the served stylesheet scopes the face to Arabic) and behavioural for the too-narrow one (Persian text on the page must pull it). Each proved able to fail. **A third trap on the way**: the minifier rewrites `U+0600-06FF` as `U+6??`, so the first structural assertion went red against correct code \u2014 a gate that fails on every build gets deleted, and it would have taken the real check with it."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 422,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**THE FLOOR \u2014 parts 2 and 3 remain, and `/api/models` is the whole of what is left.** Fonts and icons are done (entry above, \u2212934 kB measured); the floor now stands at **6,642 kB**, of which `/api/models` alone is 2,304 kB and never cached. Original entry, kept for the reasoning: 7,575 kB decoded on every SvelteKit route before its own data: `/api/models` **2,304 kB**, then ~2,505 kB of chunks, `Archivo-Variable.ttf` **636 kB**, `Vazirmatn-Variable.ttf` **235 kB**, `splash.png` and `favicon.png` **166 kB each**. Server-rendering a surface removes that for that surface; fixing the floor removes it for **every route in the product, including the ones the strangler will never reach** \u2014 and the strangler's own plan keeps the chat core to the end, so those routes carry it for the whole migration. Three separable pieces, cheapest first: (1) **the fonts and icons** \u2014 subset the variable fonts, resize the icon set to the sizes actually declared, ~1.2 MB for an afternoon's work and no API change; (2) **`/api/models` on routes that do not use it** \u2014 it is fetched from the `(app)` layout boot wave (`+layout.svelte:120`), so `/notes`, `/admin/users`, `/admin/functions` and `/admin/evaluations` all pay 2.3 MB for a model list they never render; (3) **the payload itself** \u2014 it carries `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()` per row (`utils/models.py:175`), base64 avatars included, which is why it is 2,304 kB rather than tens of kB. It is also the only floor item that is **never cached** \u2014 741 kB over the wire on every single navigation while every chunk comes from cache at 0 kB."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 423,
          "tags": [
            "#backend"
          ],
          "text": "**`routers/knowledge.py:42` writes to the database during a GET, and does one query per row while it is there.** `get_knowledge` calls `Files.get_file_metadatas_by_ids` per knowledge base \u2014 the same N+1 shape `Models.get_models()` has \u2014 and when a base references a file id that no longer exists it calls `update_knowledge_data_by_id` to prune it, so a read endpoint mutates rows. Two consequences worth separating: a concurrent read storm can write, and a reader without write intent silently repairs data. Found while surveying, not caused by it. Fix it as the knowledge migration rather than before, on the same \"prune, don't port\" rule that governed Agents."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 424,
          "tags": [
            "#backend",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**A prompt stored without a leading slash can never be fetched, edited or deleted \u2014 and every IMPORTED prompt is stored that way** (found 2026-08-02 by the Prompts guard-rail, before a line of the migration was written; reproduced against a throwaway container). `Prompts.insert_new_prompt` stores `command` **verbatim**, while three routes look it up as `f\"/{command}\"` \u2014 `routers/prompts.py:99` (get), `:126` (update), `:161` (delete). So the invariant \"commands are stored with a leading slash\" is **assumed in three places and enforced in none**. Proof: `POST /prompts/create {\"command\":\"cy-probe\"}` \u2192 200, listed as `cy-probe`; `DELETE /prompts/command/cy-probe/delete` \u2192 **401 NOT_FOUND**; still listed. The same call with `/cy-slash` \u2192 200 and gone. **The path a real user takes to hit this is import**: `Prompts.svelte`'s import handler explicitly strips a leading slash before calling create (`prompt.command.charAt(0) === '/' ? slice(1) : command`), so **every prompt imported through the UI is permanently undeletable** \u2014 and the UI gives no error, it just does nothing. The production snapshot is unaffected (all 8 rows carry the slash), which is why nobody has hit it yet; an operator who exports and re-imports will. **Fix at the single write point, not the three read points**: normalise in `insert_new_prompt` and `update_prompt_by_command` so the readers' assumption becomes true by construction, plus a one-line repair for existing rows lacking the slash. Making the three readers tolerant instead would leave the storage format ambiguous and the next reader would assume again. **The no-build panel must not port this** \u2014 it normalises on lookup."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 425,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Workshop is the next surface, elevated on production numbers** (Alexander, 2026-08-01). It was scheduled mid-Phase-3 behind Users. Measured on production, it is the worst surface in the product and the only one that misses the plan's throughput budget on every axis at once: **`/workshop/models` \u2014 144 requests, 20,520 kB, 6,181 kB transferred, 32 s**; **`/workshop/knowledge` \u2014 ~140 requests, 18,203 kB, 5,257 kB transferred, 19.07 s**. Against a budget of \u2264 ~2 MB transferred and first content under 2 s, and against the conversation baseline this migration exists to beat (172 req / 7.9 MB / ~19 s), a LIST OF MODELS is slower than a conversation. That makes it the strongest available evidence for the plan's second leg, which so far rests on the chat route alone. 5,567 lines across models/knowledge/prompts; the table predicts ~50\u201365%. **Read, not yet measured** \u2014 the last two times this was assumed the assumption was wrong (the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs both turned out already lazy), so these are candidates and the first task is a before-measurement: the route awaits `getModels` (the provider probe) _before_ rendering `<Models />`, which only then fetches `getWorkshopModels` + `getGroups` + `getBranding`, so the waterfall is serial across a component boundary; `Models.svelte` statically imports `marked`, `sortablejs` and `file-saver`; `Knowledge.svelte` statically imports `fuse.js` and `dayjs` + `relativeTime`; and every row renders `model.meta.profile_image_url`, which in this lineage is often a base64 data URI and would inflate the payload rather than the request count."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 426,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**The list payload repeats the same user avatar once per agent \u2014 MEASURED on a production snapshot, and it is not the defect I first filed** (2026-08-01). Alexander: _\"Maybe use the backup for realistic tests\"_ \u2014 right, and it overturned the hypothesis below within one query. The agent-avatar theory does not survive contact with `tools/db_snapshots/webui.db.sqlite`: of its 324 `model` rows only **16** carry a data URI at all, and all agent `meta` totals **868 kB**. Not 20 MB, not close. **What the workshop list actually costs there** \u2014 and note the filter, because `Models.get_models()` returns only `base_model_id IS NOT NULL`, so most of those 324 rows are base models and never appear: **48 agents, 1,032 kB of row data, plus 475 kB of embedded owner avatars \u2248 1.5 MB.** **The 475 kB is the interesting part, because the page renders none of it.** `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which nests the owner's whole `UserResponse` in every row, and 21 of the instance's 32 users carry a base64 avatar \u2014 those users hold **120 kB** between them, amplified ~4\u00d7 by being repeated per agent they own. The row displays \"By {name}\" as text, so roughly a third of the response is downloaded, parsed and dropped. A server-rendered page does not reduce that cost, it **does not incur it**: the row context holds a name string. First place in this migration where the fragment path shows up as bytes rather than lines. **Also found**: `Models.get_models()` calls `Users.get_user_by_id()` inside its loop \u2014 one query per agent, so 49 queries for 48 rows. **What is still NOT explained is the gap to 20 MB**, and guessing at it twice is enough: boot the snapshot with `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` and measure the real page. 48 rows also means 48 tooltips and 48 `marked.parse()` calls, and the conversation baseline is already 13.4 MB decoded before this page adds anything. **MEASURED IN A BROWSER 2026-08-02 \u2014 the mechanism is named and the 20 MB does not fully reproduce.** `cypress/e2e/upgrade/workshop-payload.cy.ts` against the snapshot (51 agents): the SPA page is **126 requests, 3,604 kB over the wire, 9,174 kB decoded, 5.4 s to the first row**. Production was 144 req / 6,181 kB transferred / 20,520 kB / 32 s \u2014 the request count lands within 13%, the bytes at roughly half, which is what more agents and larger avatars look like rather than a mechanism we have not found. **What the bytes are, in order:** `/api/models?` **2,304 kB** and `/api/v1/models/` **1,503 kB** \u2014 3,807 kB, **41% of the entire page load**, two endpoints carrying the same base64 for one list; then 2,505 kB of SvelteKit chunks, a **636 kB variable font**, 282 kB of CSS, and a **166 kB favicon** with a 166 kB splash beside it. The double-fetch on the board was real and mis-stated: not one endpoint twice, two endpoints once each. **The same page server-rendered: 11 requests, 234 kB wire, 152 ms** \u2014 and 7 kB / 44 ms on a repeat visit, because nothing is inline any more (0 data URIs against 66 in the JSON, 6 cacheable avatar URLs instead). **The comparison that does not flatter us, and the one that matches what we actually feel**: arriving at the route inside an ALREADY-BOOTED app still costs 919 kB wire \u2014 914 kB of it `/api/v1/models/`, re-downloaded with every avatar inlined on every visit \u2014 and **1,504 ms before the first row appears**, against 234 kB / 152 ms cold for the whole server-rendered document and 44 ms on a repeat. **That figure exists because Alexander disbelieved a blank cell.** The first draft of this measurement reported no time for the in-app case: `Date.now()` in a Cypress test body is stamped when the commands are QUEUED, not when they run, so the number covered the app's whole boot plus a deliberate 3-second settle, and the fallback `responseEnd` was relative to the chat page's `timeOrigin` and carried the boot too. Both were wrong by more than the thing being measured, so the cell was dashed out \u2014 and an empty cell beside 919 kB reads as \"too fast to matter\" when the bytes say the opposite. Timing is now stamped inside the browser at the click instant (`performance.now()`), which is the only clock that cannot include Cypress's queue. **The lesson is the one this board keeps relearning from the other direction**: a measurement you decline to publish is still a claim, and \"no number\" is the one reading nobody checks. Caveat kept in the open: page 1 is 24 rows against the SPA's 51, so both pages together is the like-for-like figure \u2014 22 requests, ~477 kB wire, still **7.6\u00d7 less wire and 5.7\u00d7 fewer requests**."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 427,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#dx"
          ],
          "text": "**The parity gate earned its keep on the Agents page: the empty state dropped the list container** (2026-08-02, first run against the no-build implementation). The template branched `{% if agents %}<ul data-cy=\"agents-list\">\u2026{% else %}<p data-cy=\"agents-empty\">`, so on an instance with no agents the SvelteKit page kept its empty `<ul>` and the no-build page had none. **`workshop-agents.cy.ts` waits on `agents-list` in `beforeEach`** \u2014 it passes only because it seeds three agents first, so the failure was invisible to every behavioural spec and visible to the gate that reads hooks instead of assertions. Fixed by subtraction: one container always, the empty message moved inside it as an `<li>`, and the `{% else %}` branch deleted. Parity 8/8, Agents 9/9 against the no-build page."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 428,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**A 166 kB favicon, a 166 kB splash and a 636 kB font ride every cold page load \u2014 and this reopens a call we closed as \"not perf\"** (found 2026-08-02 in the top-25 of the workshop measurement above). The `/icons/` audit at line 395 above closed the six identical 170 kB PNGs as branding art on the grounds that the image only ships 1.3 MB of icons. That judged the file on disk. **The browser fetches `/static/icons/favicon.png` and `/static/icons/splash.png` \u2014 332 kB decoded, and the wire cost is the same because PNG does not compress again** \u2014 on a cold load of any page, plus `Archivo-Variable.ttf` at 636 kB unsubsetted. Just under 1 MB before the application has rendered anything, on both the SPA and the server-rendered pages. A favicon is 16\u201348 px: this one is placeholder art at full resolution wearing the filename. Cheap and separable from the migration \u2014 resize the icon set to the sizes actually declared, and subset the font, which line 105 already proposes automating for the Cypress fixture copy."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 429,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "~~**Agent avatars are uncompressed PNG data URIs inline in the list payload**~~ \u2014 **REFUTED 2026-08-01 by the snapshot above, and kept because being wrong here is the third time this exact way.** The reading was correct as far as it went: `ModelEditor.svelte:366` really does store avatars with `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 no arguments, so PNG \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250 for a 44px slot, and `meta` really does ride whole in the list response. The arithmetic was correct too: one such avatar measures 172 kB of PNG, 230 kB as base64. **What was wrong was assuming the worst case is the common case.** Real instances have a few avatars, not one per agent. This now joins the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs as inferences that read perfectly and measured false \u2014 and all three were caught the same way, by measuring before acting. Keep the format fix (`toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` and dimensions matched to the slot) as a small, cheap, separate item; it is right, it is just not the 20 MB. #frontend #perf `ModelEditor.svelte:366` stores the avatar as `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 **no arguments, so it defaults to PNG** \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250, then writes it into `info.meta.profile_image_url`. `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which carries `meta` whole with no projection, so **every avatar rides in every list response**, and the list renders it at 44px. Measured cost of one such avatar, photo-like worst case: **172.1 kB of PNG \u2192 229.5 kB as base64 (+33%)**; 40 agents is 8.96 MB and 60 is 13.45 MB in a single response, against a page measured at 20,520 kB. A flat logo compresses far better, so the real figure depends on what people upload \u2014 **the decisive test is one `curl` against production: the byte size of `/api/v1/models/` and the agent count.** Three faults compounding: the format (a `toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` is roughly an order of magnitude smaller), the dimensions (250\u00d7250 stored for a 44px slot), and the storage shape (inline, so it can never be cached separately, lazy-loaded, or served conditionally). **The third one follows the surface**: server-rendering this page moves the base64 out of JSON and into the HTML rather than removing it, so a migration that ports it faithfully would ship the same 20 MB with better line counts. Fix it AS the migration \u2014 \"prune, don't port\". **Checked, and it is worse than a workshop problem: the avatars ride in TWO endpoints and this page fetches both.** `utils/models.py:175` sets `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()`, so `/api/models` carries the whole custom model including the base64 \u2014 and that is the endpoint the app-wide boot ladder calls on **every page**, not just this one. `/api/v1/models/` carries it a second time for the workshop list. So the conversation baseline (172 req / 7.9 MB) is probably paying this too, and `/workshop/models` pays it **twice per load**: once in the route's `onMount` and once in `Models.svelte`. That reframes the fix as an app-wide payload change with the workshop page as its worst case, and it is worth its own before/after independent of the migration."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 430,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Avatars: right-size them at the encoder, and serve them from a cacheable URL \u2014 30\u00d7 measured, no new dependency** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"for avatars we should think about making it so that they are cached at various sizes. We can really go small and fast this way.\"_ Right about the direction; the numbers say **one** right size beats a multi-size cache here). Measured on three real avatars pulled from the production snapshot, all stored 250\u00d7250: an agent avatar is **106.8 / 97.7 / 95.0 kB** because it is PNG; the same image as JPEG is **21 kB**, at 128px **5.9\u20137.2 kB**, at 88px **3.6\u20134.5 kB**. So format alone is **5\u00d7** and format-plus-size is **30\u00d7**. **The root cause is three copies of one function.** `ModelEditor.svelte:366`, `Settings/Account.svelte:134` and `ArenaModelModal.svelte:202` are the same canvas-resize block pasted three times at the same 250\u00d7250 \u2014 and only the agent one calls `toDataURL()` with **no format argument**, so it defaults to PNG while the other two pass `'image/jpeg'`. That is the written-twice drift this migration exists to delete, showing up as a 5\u00d7 payload difference between two avatars a user cannot tell apart. Fix at the encoder: one shared helper, WebP with a JPEG fallback. **DECIDED 2026-08-01 (Alexander): migration AND serve-time cache, not the encoder-only fix I first recommended.** That call is right and my framing was wrong in a way worth writing down \u2014 I argued it as either/or on payload size, but **the migration's real job is not shrinking, it is getting the blob out of the database row.** Encoder-only leaves the image inside `meta`, where it rides every response that returns `meta`, cannot be cached separately, and bloats every row and backup; and picking one size at upload is destructive, so a later size needs the original that no longer exists. The two halves compose: the migration moves originals to content-addressed storage on the data volume and leaves a reference in the row; the cache derives sizes beside them on first request; a future size costs nothing. **I also overstated the security cost** \u2014 the server already ingests and stores these images through an authenticated POST, so adding a decode raises the surface rather than opening it. Standard hardening covers it: a format allowlist, a byte ceiling, and `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. Pillow is still a genuine new dependency, verified absent from `requirements.txt` and from the built image. **The trap that must land WITH the migration, not after: exports break.** `Models.svelte:167` and `admin/Settings/Models.svelte:68` both `JSON.stringify` the model objects verbatim, so an export is self-contained today \u2014 a data URI travels with it and imports anywhere. Replace that with a local path and every export becomes non-portable, silently, and only when someone imports it into another instance. The export paths have to re-inline from storage. **Design constraints, each earned:** sizes come from a CLOSED set (44/128/256), never a free query parameter, or `?s=1..2000` is render amplification that fills the volume; the cache lives on the volume rather than in memory, because multi-worker is on the roadmap and an in-process cache would duplicate per worker; the migration is non-destructive (write the file, verify, then clear the row), idempotent, and fails closed when the volume is not writable \u2014 `data_not_writable` is already a diagnostics issue_type. `make upgrade_gate` boots a real snapshot and is the proof."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 431,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#brand"
          ],
          "text": "**Rename `/workshop/models` \u2192 `/workshop/agents`** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"should really be agents\"_). The UI **already says Agents** \u2014 `Models.svelte` renders `$i18n.t('Agents')` as the heading, the page title, \"Import Agents\" and \"Export Agents\"; only the URL, the directory and the component names still say models, so this is finishing a rename someone started rather than beginning one. What the surface holds is not models \u2014 it is a model plus a system prompt plus knowledge plus tools, which is an agent preset. Consistent with the locked 2026-07-11 decision for **one tiered \"Agents\" surface**, where extended (Sprig-delivered) agents appear alongside these with a tier badge; that decision's \"no renames\" clause was about not splitting the tiers into separate surfaces, not about keeping this name. Do it AS the migration rather than before it \u2014 the route is being rebuilt anyway, and renaming twice costs two redirect layers. Needs: the route, the nav label, the i18n keys, and a redirect from the old path for anyone holding a bookmark."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 432,
          "tags": [
            "#biomes",
            "#ratchet"
          ],
          "text": "Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 433,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 434,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 435,
          "tags": [
            "#brand"
          ],
          "text": "Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor"
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "9170c74865930531",
      "line_num": 417,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phases 3\u20134 \u2014 medium surfaces, shell flip, then biomes: workshop FIRST (see below), then users, evals, settings tabs, chat list \u2192 prune biomes (Functions, then chat core last, gated on the spike memo). THAT ORDER SORTS ON 2\u20136% OF THE COST \u2014 measured 2026-08-02, see the survey below. Reorder it.**\n- [ ] **SURVEYED: every migration candidate on one snapshot, and the sorting key was wrong** (2026-08-02; Alexander: _\"Given how well this worked let's think about what other routes would benefit from our approach.\"_). `cypress/e2e/upgrade/route-payload.cy.ts`, 10/10 green, 51 agents / 32 users / 17 knowledge bases / 8 prompts on the restored snapshot. **The finding is the FLOOR.** `/notes` for a user who owns none \u2014 notes are scoped per user and the snapshot's 14 belong to six other people \u2014 still costs **127 requests, 781 kB wire, 7,575 kB decoded**. That is what a route with *no data of its own* pays. Every route's own data measured against it: **prompts +121 kB, functions +206, users +296, knowledge +455, evaluations +1,269, agents +1,714, chat +5,170.** So for six of the eight, the list the surface exists to show is **2\u20136% of what loading it costs**, and ordering the migration by list size orders it by noise. **`/api/models` is 2,304 kB and it is fetched on every single route** \u2014 chat, notes, functions, users, all of them \u2014 and it is the only thing on the floor that is not cached: 741 kB of the 781 kB wire, **95%**, on a warm client. Someone opening `/admin/functions` to look at one function downloads 2.3 MB of model metadata. **The server-rendered page for comparison: 12 requests, 234 kB wire, 472 kB decoded \u2014 one sixteenth of the EMPTY SvelteKit route.** **Method notes, because they bound what these numbers mean**: single run per route on one machine, so decoded bytes are the ranking column (stable to 0.1 kB across three runs) and times are indicative only (knowledge came in at 1,425 ms and 3,241 ms on two runs); the shell is warm after the first route, so `wireKB` is a returning-visitor figure, not a cold one \u2014 `workshop-payload.cy.ts` holds the cold numbers. `/home` is **not** in the survey: Spaces has no list hook yet, and measuring it against a shell selector would have produced a plausible number for the wrong thing. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **The reordering that follows from it \u2014 one column is Alexander's, not mine.** Because the throughput win is within a few hundred kB of identical for every candidate, throughput can no longer break the tie; what is left is lines deleted and how often a route is opened. **There is no telemetry in this product, so the last column is judgement and stays empty until Alexander fills it.** Component lines, counted: **users 3,126 \u00b7 notes 2,910 \u00b7 knowledge 1,671 \u00b7 evaluations 1,359 \u00b7 functions 1,249 \u00b7 prompts 635** (Agents landed at 53% of its count, so treat these as ceilings). On that basis, and pending the frequency column: **`/admin/users` moves ahead of knowledge** \u2014 it is the largest component in the candidate set, it is the one surface whose base64 avatars are actually *rendered* rather than downloaded and dropped, and `users.cy.ts` already exists as its guard-rail. **`/notes` is a different animal and should not be judged on this table** \u2014 2,910 lines of editor, not a list, so the props-and-forms pattern that took Agents to zero JavaScript does not obviously apply. **`/workshop/prompts` is the cheap one**: 635 lines, 121 kB above the floor, and it would take an afternoon \u2014 worth doing as a warm-up, not as a win. #frontend\n- [x] **Floor, part 1 of 3 \u2014 fonts and icons: \u2212934 kB decoded on EVERY route, measured before and after** (2026-08-02; Alexander: _\"We could quickly do A and B.\"_). Three samples each side through `route-payload.cy.ts`, **spread 0.0 kB**, so the delta is the measurement rather than an impression: the floor went **7,576.0 \u2192 6,642.5 kB** and every SvelteKit route moved by the same \u2212934, agents-legacy by \u22121,056. The server-rendered pages moved 0.0 \u2014 they load none of it, which is the check that the instrument is reading what it claims. **Fonts.** Four of five `@font-face` rules shipped `.ttf`; `Mona-Sans.woff2` had been sitting beside them the whole time proving the toolchain could do better. Archivo **652 \u2192 187 kB (\u221271%)** and Vazirmatn **241 \u2192 111 kB**, by format conversion only \u2014 **not one glyph dropped**, so no locale audit was owed. Then a `unicode-range` scoping Vazirmatn to the Arabic blocks: it sat in the body stack as Archivo's fallback, so every reader in every locale was pulling 235 kB of Persian glyphs to render English. **It is now fetched on no route at all** unless Persian text is present. **Icons.** The `image/svg+xml` link is gone and nothing was lost: `favicon.svg` is 203 kB of **two 2036\u00d72040 PNGs base64-embedded in an SVG wrapper**, and the two embedded images are byte-identical (md5 `1dfc08a3b1e1`, 75,512 bytes each) \u2014 a `<style>` block switching light/dark between the same picture, preferred by browsers over the 13.6 kB PNG already declared beside it. The five 512\u00d7512 masters (`favicon`, `logo`, `splash` and both \"dark\" twins, all byte-identical to each other) are now 256\u00d7256 at **45,710 bytes**. **Why the asset and not the 27 call sites**: it is one cached URL today, so repointing some of them would make the browser fetch two files instead of one. Largest render anywhere is 5rem, the splash is 6rem, and the PWA manifest uses separate 192/512 files \u2014 nothing needed 512. `git checkout` reverts it if the art matters more than the bytes. **The honest limit**: these assets cache and `/api/models` does not, so this is a first-visit win, not a second-navigation one. #frontend #perf\n- [x] **The `unicode-range` got a two-sided guard, and writing it taught me the two directions need different assertions** (`cypress/e2e/font-unicode-range.cy.ts`). It can fail two ways and **only one is visible**: too narrow and Persian readers silently get fallback glyphs, which nobody here reads and which would ship; too wide and the 235 kB quietly returns. **Two wrong attempts, both worth keeping.** (1) Widening the range to include Latin did *not* re-introduce the download \u2014 `unicode-range` says which characters a face MAY serve, but a browser only fetches a face it actually USES, and Archivo comes first in the stack. (2) Deleting the range entirely did not either, on a page reachable without signing in. So the first draft's \"en-US does not download Vazirmatn\" **passed whether or not the change was present** \u2014 an assertion that cannot fail, reporting coverage it did not have. Rebuilt as structural for the too-wide direction (assert the served stylesheet scopes the face to Arabic) and behavioural for the too-narrow one (Persian text on the page must pull it). Each proved able to fail. **A third trap on the way**: the minifier rewrites `U+0600-06FF` as `U+6??`, so the first structural assertion went red against correct code \u2014 a gate that fails on every build gets deleted, and it would have taken the real check with it. #frontend #dx\n- [ ] **THE FLOOR \u2014 parts 2 and 3 remain, and `/api/models` is the whole of what is left.** Fonts and icons are done (entry above, \u2212934 kB measured); the floor now stands at **6,642 kB**, of which `/api/models` alone is 2,304 kB and never cached. Original entry, kept for the reasoning: 7,575 kB decoded on every SvelteKit route before its own data: `/api/models` **2,304 kB**, then ~2,505 kB of chunks, `Archivo-Variable.ttf` **636 kB**, `Vazirmatn-Variable.ttf` **235 kB**, `splash.png` and `favicon.png` **166 kB each**. Server-rendering a surface removes that for that surface; fixing the floor removes it for **every route in the product, including the ones the strangler will never reach** \u2014 and the strangler's own plan keeps the chat core to the end, so those routes carry it for the whole migration. Three separable pieces, cheapest first: (1) **the fonts and icons** \u2014 subset the variable fonts, resize the icon set to the sizes actually declared, ~1.2 MB for an afternoon's work and no API change; (2) **`/api/models` on routes that do not use it** \u2014 it is fetched from the `(app)` layout boot wave (`+layout.svelte:120`), so `/notes`, `/admin/users`, `/admin/functions` and `/admin/evaluations` all pay 2.3 MB for a model list they never render; (3) **the payload itself** \u2014 it carries `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()` per row (`utils/models.py:175`), base64 avatars included, which is why it is 2,304 kB rather than tens of kB. It is also the only floor item that is **never cached** \u2014 741 kB over the wire on every single navigation while every chunk comes from cache at 0 kB. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **`routers/knowledge.py:42` writes to the database during a GET, and does one query per row while it is there.** `get_knowledge` calls `Files.get_file_metadatas_by_ids` per knowledge base \u2014 the same N+1 shape `Models.get_models()` has \u2014 and when a base references a file id that no longer exists it calls `update_knowledge_data_by_id` to prune it, so a read endpoint mutates rows. Two consequences worth separating: a concurrent read storm can write, and a reader without write intent silently repairs data. Found while surveying, not caused by it. Fix it as the knowledge migration rather than before, on the same \"prune, don't port\" rule that governed Agents. #backend\n- [ ] **A prompt stored without a leading slash can never be fetched, edited or deleted \u2014 and every IMPORTED prompt is stored that way** (found 2026-08-02 by the Prompts guard-rail, before a line of the migration was written; reproduced against a throwaway container). `Prompts.insert_new_prompt` stores `command` **verbatim**, while three routes look it up as `f\"/{command}\"` \u2014 `routers/prompts.py:99` (get), `:126` (update), `:161` (delete). So the invariant \"commands are stored with a leading slash\" is **assumed in three places and enforced in none**. Proof: `POST /prompts/create {\"command\":\"cy-probe\"}` \u2192 200, listed as `cy-probe`; `DELETE /prompts/command/cy-probe/delete` \u2192 **401 NOT_FOUND**; still listed. The same call with `/cy-slash` \u2192 200 and gone. **The path a real user takes to hit this is import**: `Prompts.svelte`'s import handler explicitly strips a leading slash before calling create (`prompt.command.charAt(0) === '/' ? slice(1) : command`), so **every prompt imported through the UI is permanently undeletable** \u2014 and the UI gives no error, it just does nothing. The production snapshot is unaffected (all 8 rows carry the slash), which is why nobody has hit it yet; an operator who exports and re-imports will. **Fix at the single write point, not the three read points**: normalise in `insert_new_prompt` and `update_prompt_by_command` so the readers' assumption becomes true by construction, plus a one-line repair for existing rows lacking the slash. Making the three readers tolerant instead would leave the storage format ambiguous and the next reader would assume again. **The no-build panel must not port this** \u2014 it normalises on lookup. #backend #critical\n- [ ] **Workshop is the next surface, elevated on production numbers** (Alexander, 2026-08-01). It was scheduled mid-Phase-3 behind Users. Measured on production, it is the worst surface in the product and the only one that misses the plan's throughput budget on every axis at once: **`/workshop/models` \u2014 144 requests, 20,520 kB, 6,181 kB transferred, 32 s**; **`/workshop/knowledge` \u2014 ~140 requests, 18,203 kB, 5,257 kB transferred, 19.07 s**. Against a budget of \u2264 ~2 MB transferred and first content under 2 s, and against the conversation baseline this migration exists to beat (172 req / 7.9 MB / ~19 s), a LIST OF MODELS is slower than a conversation. That makes it the strongest available evidence for the plan's second leg, which so far rests on the chat route alone. 5,567 lines across models/knowledge/prompts; the table predicts ~50\u201365%. **Read, not yet measured** \u2014 the last two times this was assumed the assumption was wrong (the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs both turned out already lazy), so these are candidates and the first task is a before-measurement: the route awaits `getModels` (the provider probe) _before_ rendering `<Models />`, which only then fetches `getWorkshopModels` + `getGroups` + `getBranding`, so the waterfall is serial across a component boundary; `Models.svelte` statically imports `marked`, `sortablejs` and `file-saver`; `Knowledge.svelte` statically imports `fuse.js` and `dayjs` + `relativeTime`; and every row renders `model.meta.profile_image_url`, which in this lineage is often a base64 data URI and would inflate the payload rather than the request count. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **The list payload repeats the same user avatar once per agent \u2014 MEASURED on a production snapshot, and it is not the defect I first filed** (2026-08-01). Alexander: _\"Maybe use the backup for realistic tests\"_ \u2014 right, and it overturned the hypothesis below within one query. The agent-avatar theory does not survive contact with `tools/db_snapshots/webui.db.sqlite`: of its 324 `model` rows only **16** carry a data URI at all, and all agent `meta` totals **868 kB**. Not 20 MB, not close. **What the workshop list actually costs there** \u2014 and note the filter, because `Models.get_models()` returns only `base_model_id IS NOT NULL`, so most of those 324 rows are base models and never appear: **48 agents, 1,032 kB of row data, plus 475 kB of embedded owner avatars \u2248 1.5 MB.** **The 475 kB is the interesting part, because the page renders none of it.** `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which nests the owner's whole `UserResponse` in every row, and 21 of the instance's 32 users carry a base64 avatar \u2014 those users hold **120 kB** between them, amplified ~4\u00d7 by being repeated per agent they own. The row displays \"By {name}\" as text, so roughly a third of the response is downloaded, parsed and dropped. A server-rendered page does not reduce that cost, it **does not incur it**: the row context holds a name string. First place in this migration where the fragment path shows up as bytes rather than lines. **Also found**: `Models.get_models()` calls `Users.get_user_by_id()` inside its loop \u2014 one query per agent, so 49 queries for 48 rows. **What is still NOT explained is the gap to 20 MB**, and guessing at it twice is enough: boot the snapshot with `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` and measure the real page. 48 rows also means 48 tooltips and 48 `marked.parse()` calls, and the conversation baseline is already 13.4 MB decoded before this page adds anything. **MEASURED IN A BROWSER 2026-08-02 \u2014 the mechanism is named and the 20 MB does not fully reproduce.** `cypress/e2e/upgrade/workshop-payload.cy.ts` against the snapshot (51 agents): the SPA page is **126 requests, 3,604 kB over the wire, 9,174 kB decoded, 5.4 s to the first row**. Production was 144 req / 6,181 kB transferred / 20,520 kB / 32 s \u2014 the request count lands within 13%, the bytes at roughly half, which is what more agents and larger avatars look like rather than a mechanism we have not found. **What the bytes are, in order:** `/api/models?` **2,304 kB** and `/api/v1/models/` **1,503 kB** \u2014 3,807 kB, **41% of the entire page load**, two endpoints carrying the same base64 for one list; then 2,505 kB of SvelteKit chunks, a **636 kB variable font**, 282 kB of CSS, and a **166 kB favicon** with a 166 kB splash beside it. The double-fetch on the board was real and mis-stated: not one endpoint twice, two endpoints once each. **The same page server-rendered: 11 requests, 234 kB wire, 152 ms** \u2014 and 7 kB / 44 ms on a repeat visit, because nothing is inline any more (0 data URIs against 66 in the JSON, 6 cacheable avatar URLs instead). **The comparison that does not flatter us, and the one that matches what we actually feel**: arriving at the route inside an ALREADY-BOOTED app still costs 919 kB wire \u2014 914 kB of it `/api/v1/models/`, re-downloaded with every avatar inlined on every visit \u2014 and **1,504 ms before the first row appears**, against 234 kB / 152 ms cold for the whole server-rendered document and 44 ms on a repeat. **That figure exists because Alexander disbelieved a blank cell.** The first draft of this measurement reported no time for the in-app case: `Date.now()` in a Cypress test body is stamped when the commands are QUEUED, not when they run, so the number covered the app's whole boot plus a deliberate 3-second settle, and the fallback `responseEnd` was relative to the chat page's `timeOrigin` and carried the boot too. Both were wrong by more than the thing being measured, so the cell was dashed out \u2014 and an empty cell beside 919 kB reads as \"too fast to matter\" when the bytes say the opposite. Timing is now stamped inside the browser at the click instant (`performance.now()`), which is the only clock that cannot include Cypress's queue. **The lesson is the one this board keeps relearning from the other direction**: a measurement you decline to publish is still a claim, and \"no number\" is the one reading nobody checks. Caveat kept in the open: page 1 is 24 rows against the SPA's 51, so both pages together is the like-for-like figure \u2014 22 requests, ~477 kB wire, still **7.6\u00d7 less wire and 5.7\u00d7 fewer requests**. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [x] **The parity gate earned its keep on the Agents page: the empty state dropped the list container** (2026-08-02, first run against the no-build implementation). The template branched `{% if agents %}<ul data-cy=\"agents-list\">\u2026{% else %}<p data-cy=\"agents-empty\">`, so on an instance with no agents the SvelteKit page kept its empty `<ul>` and the no-build page had none. **`workshop-agents.cy.ts` waits on `agents-list` in `beforeEach`** \u2014 it passes only because it seeds three agents first, so the failure was invisible to every behavioural spec and visible to the gate that reads hooks instead of assertions. Fixed by subtraction: one container always, the empty message moved inside it as an `<li>`, and the `{% else %}` branch deleted. Parity 8/8, Agents 9/9 against the no-build page. #frontend #dx\n- [ ] **A 166 kB favicon, a 166 kB splash and a 636 kB font ride every cold page load \u2014 and this reopens a call we closed as \"not perf\"** (found 2026-08-02 in the top-25 of the workshop measurement above). The `/icons/` audit at line 395 above closed the six identical 170 kB PNGs as branding art on the grounds that the image only ships 1.3 MB of icons. That judged the file on disk. **The browser fetches `/static/icons/favicon.png` and `/static/icons/splash.png` \u2014 332 kB decoded, and the wire cost is the same because PNG does not compress again** \u2014 on a cold load of any page, plus `Archivo-Variable.ttf` at 636 kB unsubsetted. Just under 1 MB before the application has rendered anything, on both the SPA and the server-rendered pages. A favicon is 16\u201348 px: this one is placeholder art at full resolution wearing the filename. Cheap and separable from the migration \u2014 resize the icon set to the sizes actually declared, and subset the font, which line 105 already proposes automating for the Cypress fixture copy. #frontend #perf\n- [ ] ~~**Agent avatars are uncompressed PNG data URIs inline in the list payload**~~ \u2014 **REFUTED 2026-08-01 by the snapshot above, and kept because being wrong here is the third time this exact way.** The reading was correct as far as it went: `ModelEditor.svelte:366` really does store avatars with `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 no arguments, so PNG \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250 for a 44px slot, and `meta` really does ride whole in the list response. The arithmetic was correct too: one such avatar measures 172 kB of PNG, 230 kB as base64. **What was wrong was assuming the worst case is the common case.** Real instances have a few avatars, not one per agent. This now joins the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs as inferences that read perfectly and measured false \u2014 and all three were caught the same way, by measuring before acting. Keep the format fix (`toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` and dimensions matched to the slot) as a small, cheap, separate item; it is right, it is just not the 20 MB. #frontend #perf `ModelEditor.svelte:366` stores the avatar as `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 **no arguments, so it defaults to PNG** \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250, then writes it into `info.meta.profile_image_url`. `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which carries `meta` whole with no projection, so **every avatar rides in every list response**, and the list renders it at 44px. Measured cost of one such avatar, photo-like worst case: **172.1 kB of PNG \u2192 229.5 kB as base64 (+33%)**; 40 agents is 8.96 MB and 60 is 13.45 MB in a single response, against a page measured at 20,520 kB. A flat logo compresses far better, so the real figure depends on what people upload \u2014 **the decisive test is one `curl` against production: the byte size of `/api/v1/models/` and the agent count.** Three faults compounding: the format (a `toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` is roughly an order of magnitude smaller), the dimensions (250\u00d7250 stored for a 44px slot), and the storage shape (inline, so it can never be cached separately, lazy-loaded, or served conditionally). **The third one follows the surface**: server-rendering this page moves the base64 out of JSON and into the HTML rather than removing it, so a migration that ports it faithfully would ship the same 20 MB with better line counts. Fix it AS the migration \u2014 \"prune, don't port\". **Checked, and it is worse than a workshop problem: the avatars ride in TWO endpoints and this page fetches both.** `utils/models.py:175` sets `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()`, so `/api/models` carries the whole custom model including the base64 \u2014 and that is the endpoint the app-wide boot ladder calls on **every page**, not just this one. `/api/v1/models/` carries it a second time for the workshop list. So the conversation baseline (172 req / 7.9 MB) is probably paying this too, and `/workshop/models` pays it **twice per load**: once in the route's `onMount` and once in `Models.svelte`. That reframes the fix as an app-wide payload change with the workshop page as its worst case, and it is worth its own before/after independent of the migration. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **Avatars: right-size them at the encoder, and serve them from a cacheable URL \u2014 30\u00d7 measured, no new dependency** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"for avatars we should think about making it so that they are cached at various sizes. We can really go small and fast this way.\"_ Right about the direction; the numbers say **one** right size beats a multi-size cache here). Measured on three real avatars pulled from the production snapshot, all stored 250\u00d7250: an agent avatar is **106.8 / 97.7 / 95.0 kB** because it is PNG; the same image as JPEG is **21 kB**, at 128px **5.9\u20137.2 kB**, at 88px **3.6\u20134.5 kB**. So format alone is **5\u00d7** and format-plus-size is **30\u00d7**. **The root cause is three copies of one function.** `ModelEditor.svelte:366`, `Settings/Account.svelte:134` and `ArenaModelModal.svelte:202` are the same canvas-resize block pasted three times at the same 250\u00d7250 \u2014 and only the agent one calls `toDataURL()` with **no format argument**, so it defaults to PNG while the other two pass `'image/jpeg'`. That is the written-twice drift this migration exists to delete, showing up as a 5\u00d7 payload difference between two avatars a user cannot tell apart. Fix at the encoder: one shared helper, WebP with a JPEG fallback. **DECIDED 2026-08-01 (Alexander): migration AND serve-time cache, not the encoder-only fix I first recommended.** That call is right and my framing was wrong in a way worth writing down \u2014 I argued it as either/or on payload size, but **the migration's real job is not shrinking, it is getting the blob out of the database row.** Encoder-only leaves the image inside `meta`, where it rides every response that returns `meta`, cannot be cached separately, and bloats every row and backup; and picking one size at upload is destructive, so a later size needs the original that no longer exists. The two halves compose: the migration moves originals to content-addressed storage on the data volume and leaves a reference in the row; the cache derives sizes beside them on first request; a future size costs nothing. **I also overstated the security cost** \u2014 the server already ingests and stores these images through an authenticated POST, so adding a decode raises the surface rather than opening it. Standard hardening covers it: a format allowlist, a byte ceiling, and `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. Pillow is still a genuine new dependency, verified absent from `requirements.txt` and from the built image. **The trap that must land WITH the migration, not after: exports break.** `Models.svelte:167` and `admin/Settings/Models.svelte:68` both `JSON.stringify` the model objects verbatim, so an export is self-contained today \u2014 a data URI travels with it and imports anywhere. Replace that with a local path and every export becomes non-portable, silently, and only when someone imports it into another instance. The export paths have to re-inline from storage. **Design constraints, each earned:** sizes come from a CLOSED set (44/128/256), never a free query parameter, or `?s=1..2000` is render amplification that fills the volume; the cache lives on the volume rather than in memory, because multi-worker is on the roadmap and an in-process cache would duplicate per worker; the migration is non-destructive (write the file, verify, then clear the row), idempotent, and fails closed when the volume is not writable \u2014 `data_not_writable` is already a diagnostics issue_type. `make upgrade_gate` boots a real snapshot and is the proof. #frontend #perf\n- [ ] **Rename `/workshop/models` \u2192 `/workshop/agents`** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"should really be agents\"_). The UI **already says Agents** \u2014 `Models.svelte` renders `$i18n.t('Agents')` as the heading, the page title, \"Import Agents\" and \"Export Agents\"; only the URL, the directory and the component names still say models, so this is finishing a rename someone started rather than beginning one. What the surface holds is not models \u2014 it is a model plus a system prompt plus knowledge plus tools, which is an agent preset. Consistent with the locked 2026-07-11 decision for **one tiered \"Agents\" surface**, where extended (Sprig-delivered) agents appear alongside these with a tier badge; that decision's \"no renames\" clause was about not splitting the tiers into separate surfaces, not about keeping this name. Do it AS the migration rather than before it \u2014 the route is being rebuilt anyway, and renaming twice costs two redirect layers. Needs: the route, the nav label, the i18n keys, and a redirect from the old path for anyone holding a bookmark. #frontend #brand\n- [ ] Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases #biomes #ratchet\n- [ ] **Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface. #frontend\n- [ ] Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free\n- [ ] Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor #brand\n\n`TODO.md:L417`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5550
    },
