      ],
      "text": "**Phase 0 \u2014 seam + Sprigs pilot SHIPPED, and it did NOT shrink the code (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 31 tests/30 passing/1 pending by design; committed). The seam: `sage_is_ai/pages/` included in `main.py` at `/pages`, with `/pages/_assets` on `CachedStaticFiles`, both registered BEFORE the SPA catch-all so the compiled bundle never sees them. `/themes/active.css` already relied on that ordering; naming it makes it a seam rather than a coincidence. Assets get a week-long cache plus `?v=VERSION` busting, because there is no build step to content-hash them \u2014 which is the point. The pilot: `/pages/admin/sprigs` talks straight to `/api/v1/retrieval/sprigs/{catalog,graft,prune}` instead of through the generated wrapper layer. The proof: `sprigs-panel.cy.ts` green against both \u2014 7/7 legacy, 8/8 no-build \u2014 and the extra test is the one that stops it passing vacuously, asserting no `_app/immutable` script is on the page. The uncomfortable measurement: predicted 238 \u2192 ~150; measured Svelte 215 + ~16 wrapper against island 189 + 44 route = 233, so the pilot came out slightly BIGGER per surface. Why that is not a refutation: this panel is a list with buttons that POST and re-fetch, so by the plan's own island test it is fragment-shaped \u2014 and a fragment needs the operator's token, which lived in localStorage until the Phase 1 cookie bridge. The ISLAND path does not shrink a form surface; the FRAGMENT path is the one the estimate assumed, and it was structurally unavailable this phase. Treat the table's other rows as unmeasured until a fragment surface lands. Second limit: the island fetches with the localStorage token, so there is no first-paint win here.**\n- [x] **Re-measured with a server-rendered FRAGMENT \u2014 157 lines, a 32% cut, and the estimate is close after all** (2026-07-27; spec 10/10 against the fragment, `make e2e` 42 tests/39 passing/3 pending by design, `ui_sprig_gate` 22/22, `sprig_smoke` 66/66). Same panel, third implementation, non-comment lines: **Svelte 231** (215 + ~16 wrapper) \u00b7 **island 233** (189 + 44) \u00b7 **fragment 157** (109 render + 48 routes, and **zero** client JS beyond one shared vendored htmx), against a plan predicting 238 \u2192 ~150. **The first fragment draft was 208, and that number was wrong about the method rather than the approach.** It came down by three things, all found by asking whether it could be cleaner: two near-identical Graft/Prune branches collapsed into one described button; the capability and the action verb moved out of the request and into the path, so the two lifecycle endpoints became one with a `Literal[\"graft\",\"prune\"]` FastAPI refuses off-list (the guard-rail asserts the 422); and a hardcoded table of prune-reset sentences moved into the prune response as `messages`, which is the real fix, since the Svelte panel and the island each carried their own copy. **A first draft of a migrated surface is not its measurement.** Roughly half the remaining 109 lines are hand-built HTML f-strings, so the floor is ~157 by hand and plausibly ~110 templated. The island is deleted; three implementations of one panel is the duplication this migration exists to remove. htmx 2.0.4 vendored with a provenance chain recorded \u2014 note for the next person: npm's `dist.integrity` is a TARBALL digest and will never match a file's own hash. #frontend\n- [x] **Add a template engine and re-measure a third time** (DONE by the twelve-panel Jinja2 conversion, 2026-08-01 \u2014 totals live in the archived entry) \u2014 ~150 of the fragment's 208 lines are hand-built HTML strings. jinja2 is not in the image (checked), so it is a real dependency decision, but it now has a number attached rather than a preference: it is the difference between a 10% cut and the plan's 40%. Do it when the second and third fragment surfaces land, so the choice is made against three call sites and not one. #frontend\n\n`#frontend` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L407`",
      "type": "text",
