      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 412,
          "tags": [
            "#sprigs",
            "#ui"
          ],
          "text": "**ui-Sprig NAMED SLOTS \u2014 decided 2026-08-01, not built** (Alexander, seeing the workshop-welcome fragment on the diagnostics page: _\"I'm not so sure we should have [it] everywhere\"_). Right, and the spec said so first: `sprig-spec/v1.md:137` already describes extension through \"mounts, **slots**, or a declarative manifest\" \u2014 plural, with a manifest \u2014 while the implementation ships **one unnamed global slot** in `shell.py:117`, so a grafted fragment renders on all fourteen server-rendered pages. Verified on the review instance: every one of the six checked showed it. An implementation-behind-spec divergence, so it belongs in `IMPLEMENTATION.md`. **Chosen design**: the rootstock declares the slot set, the Sprig's manifest names one, and the validator refuses an unknown name \u2014 fail-closed, the same shape as the 22 refusals `make ui_sprig_gate` already proves. The author knows where their fragment belongs better than the operator does, which is why this beat \"admin picks at graft\". **The coupling nobody had noticed, and it decides the sequencing**: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_UI` is a SINGLE pointer, so only one ui-Sprig can be live at a time \u2014 named slots do not pay off until several can, and building them first is a bet on a multi-Sprig future not yet committed to. Either sequence this behind that decision or accept it as deliberately forward-looking. **Work, across two repos**: `shell.py` declares the set and `render_page` emits only the named slot; `sprigs/ui_dispatch.py` validates it; `scripts/build-sprig-ui.sh` refuses at BUILD too, since it is deliberately stricter than the runtime; `ui_sprig_gate` grows a 23rd refusal, because a validator that gains a rule needs a test proving the rule can say no; and the spec repo gains the manifest field plus a version bump. **The risk to write into the spec**: the slot set becomes a published contract, so renaming a slot later breaks every Sprig that named it \u2014 version the set, do not rename in place. The first-party `workshop-welcome` example is the test case, being page-specific by nature; that is what surfaced this at all."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 413,
          "tags": [
