      ],
      "text": "**Phase 1 \u2014 ui-Sprig contract + cookie bridge SHIPPED (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 40 tests/39 passing/1 pending by design, `make sprig_smoke` 66/66, new `make ui_sprig_gate` 22/22; committed). The cookie bridge came in far under estimate. The plan treats it as a prerequisite subsystem, but `utils/auth.get_current_user` ALREADY falls back to the `token` cookie and every sign-in path already sets it httponly, so a render route had identity for free. What was actually missing is that a PAGE must fail differently from an API: `pages/auth.py` is ~30 lines turning a refusal into a 307 to `/auth?next=<path>` \u2014 path only, since a redirect target echoed from a request is an open redirect \u2014 with a signed-in non-admin getting 403 instead, because bouncing them to sign-in would loop. A near-miss worth recording: the first draft decoded the JWT itself and within twenty lines had drifted twice \u2014 it added a session check the API path does not make, and MISSED the try.sage session-reset cutoff it does, so a facilitator pressing \"Reset now\" would have invalidated every API session while leaving these pages signed in. The audit counted authorization restated ~142 times in the frontend; a second copy in the BACKEND is the same disease with a shorter incubation. `pages-cookie-auth.cy.ts` 4/4, including the non-admin branch and that the cookie is httpOnly. The ui-Sprig contract: `sprigs/ui_dispatch.py` mirroring `theme` exactly, `SPRIG_ACTIVE_UI` / `SPRIG_UI_SCRIPTING_GRANT` pointers, `/ui/active.html`, a server-rendered slot, `POST /sprigs/ui/scripting`, and a first-party example published to the local registry. Lifecycle proven end to end against the real artifact (`ui-sprig-contract.cy.ts` 5/5). The grant is held by NAME, not as a boolean \u2014 a decision about a fragment an admin read cannot be inherited by whatever is grafted next \u2014 and both readers revalidate, so revoking works without a regraft. \"No framework sprigs\" is enforced three ways, and the memo is honest that no regex decides it: scripting off unless granted; inline-only and same-origin when granted; and a 16 kB script ceiling, sized from the runtime probe's ~18 kB minimum for an independently-built biome. `make ui_sprig_gate` proves 22 REFUSALS against the real validator, asserting the REASON rather than the outcome \u2014 and that script passed silently on its first run because `docker run` without `-i` gave python empty stdin, so it now carries a sentinel. Deliberately NOT done: an `app.html` slot for the SPA, since the slot lives in the surface that survives the migration.**\n- [ ] **ui-Sprig NAMED SLOTS \u2014 decided 2026-08-01, not built** (Alexander, seeing the workshop-welcome fragment on the diagnostics page: _\"I'm not so sure we should have [it] everywhere\"_). Right, and the spec said so first: `sprig-spec/v1.md:137` already describes extension through \"mounts, **slots**, or a declarative manifest\" \u2014 plural, with a manifest \u2014 while the implementation ships **one unnamed global slot** in `shell.py:117`, so a grafted fragment renders on all fourteen server-rendered pages. Verified on the review instance: every one of the six checked showed it. An implementation-behind-spec divergence, so it belongs in `IMPLEMENTATION.md`. **Chosen design**: the rootstock declares the slot set, the Sprig's manifest names one, and the validator refuses an unknown name \u2014 fail-closed, the same shape as the 22 refusals `make ui_sprig_gate` already proves. The author knows where their fragment belongs better than the operator does, which is why this beat \"admin picks at graft\". **The coupling nobody had noticed, and it decides the sequencing**: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_UI` is a SINGLE pointer, so only one ui-Sprig can be live at a time \u2014 named slots do not pay off until several can, and building them first is a bet on a multi-Sprig future not yet committed to. Either sequence this behind that decision or accept it as deliberately forward-looking. **Work, across two repos**: `shell.py` declares the set and `render_page` emits only the named slot; `sprigs/ui_dispatch.py` validates it; `scripts/build-sprig-ui.sh` refuses at BUILD too, since it is deliberately stricter than the runtime; `ui_sprig_gate` grows a 23rd refusal, because a validator that gains a rule needs a test proving the rule can say no; and the spec repo gains the manifest field plus a version bump. **The risk to write into the spec**: the slot set becomes a published contract, so renaming a slot later breaks every Sprig that named it \u2014 version the set, do not rename in place. The first-party `workshop-welcome` example is the test case, being page-specific by nature; that is what surfaced this at all. #sprigs #ui\n- [ ] Write \"no framework sprigs\" into the ui-Sprig spec repo \u2014 fragments + host runtime only; carry the THREE mechanisms above (grant, inline-and-same-origin, size ceiling) and the honest note that no static check decides \"is this a framework\" #sprigs #ui\n\n`#sprigs` `#ui` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L411`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
